# THE TEXT EDITOR (docs/editor-plan.md E5): the whole of what "done"
# means for v1, in one script — launch to an empty buffer, type,
# save-as to a new file, reopen a file, edit it, undo, save, summon the
# find bar and search with a regex, dismiss it, and be warned about
# unsaved work on close.
#
# THIS SCENE IS NOT A CONFORMANCE SCENE. Every other script here drives
# one feature to its edges; this one drives an APP, and its assertions
# are the ones a person would make. That is what buys the editor the
# same proof every scene gets — byte-frozen output compared across
# platforms, and check-steps demanding a leg wherever it can run. An app
# only a human has ever run is an app nobody can change safely.
#
# THE DIRTY MARK IS THE ASSERTION ALMOST EVERYWHERE, and it is a much
# stronger claim than it looks: the app declares `dirty` from
# `text != saved`, so `expect_dirty false` after an undo says the
# restored buffer is BYTE-IDENTICAL to what is on disk — a comparison of
# the whole document, made by the app, read back off the platform's own
# window chrome (the dot in the macOS close button, a leading `*` in the
# WinUI caption, a bullet in the GTK header bar).
#
# THE FILES ARE THE APP'S OWN, written before anything is shown, at
# <temp>/kaya-editor-<pid>/. App and interpreter are the same process, so
# they agree on that path with no runner involvement, and the pid keeps
# parallel legs from colliding. Only BASENAMES are named, so one script
# serves five lanes whose temp directories differ.
#
# NO EXTENSIONS ON ANY NAME, the save scene's rule and for its reason: a
# save panel publishes its name field with a known extension HIDDEN when
# the user's Finder preference says so, so `expect_save_dialog` would
# read the stem on one machine and the whole name on another. A name
# with no extension has no stem to differ from.
#
# THE DOCUMENT'S NAME IS THE WINDOW'S TITLE and appears nowhere else
# (2026-08-10). The four expect_title lines below are the whole of that
# claim: the no-destination name at launch, the name the save panel was
# given, the name the picker chose, and that name again after a plain
# Save — and the status line beneath never repeats any of them.
#
# EVERY expect_title HERE SITS AT A CLEAN MOMENT, and that is a rule and
# not a coincidence (docs/dirty-plan.md, §2 open question 2). The
# declared title is untouched by `dirty` on every backend — a marker
# composed into the app's own string is D1's named rejection — but the
# WinUI arm composes its asterisk into the RENDERED caption, which is
# exactly what expect_title reads there. A title assertion inside a
# dirty stretch is therefore a byte comparison that cannot hold on five
# lanes at once. dirty.steps carries the same sentence and the same
# discipline; this scene is the first to need it four times, which is
# why the sentence stopped being prose: tools/check-steps.sh now refuses
# any expect_title standing between an `expect_dirty true` and the next
# `expect_dirty false`. It caught three of the four lines below on the
# first run that worked — written after their save, where the app was
# genuinely clean and the SCRIPT had not said so, and a script's own
# claim is the only record a gate can read.
#
# THE EDITOR OPENS ON A BUFFER AND A STATUS LINE AND NOTHING ELSE
# (2026-08-10): sublime-shaped, the buffer filling the window, no find
# bar until somebody asks for one. So the first reading is the status
# line — label#0, the app's leftmost bottom label — and label#1, the find
# tally, is EMPTY because the bar it belongs to is not mounted.
expect label#0 "new file"
expect textarea#0 ""
expect label#1 ""
expect_dirty false
# A BUFFER WITH NO DESTINATION IS CALLED SOMETHING, and this is the word
# every editor uses for it. The window is named before the document is.
expect_title "untitled"
# THE SAME TWO LABELS READ OFF THE REAL TREE, in child order, which the
# creation-ordered registries above cannot see: one status row, the
# document's state on the left and a silent tally on the right. This is
# the assertion that fails if the find bar is ever mounted at launch
# again — a bar puts its own row between the buffer and this one, and its
# controls carry no label, so the row this reads would be the wrong one.
expect_order row#0 "new file|"

# AND THE BUFFER FILLS THE WINDOW, which is the whole of "sublime-style"
# and was the defect this app opened with: a 240x96 box in the corner of
# a 640x420 window, with every share assertion passing, because
# expect_shares reads the TRACK and the track was right all along. The
# two readings below are the two halves of "fills" that a harness can
# see: the root consumes the window's content area, and the buffer
# consumes the track its column handed it.
#
# ONE HALF IS NOT MECHANIZED HERE AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT: both verbs
# read the MAIN axis, and the buffer's WIDTH comes from `align: stretch`
# on the cross one, which no observation in the harness reads
# (expect_aligned cannot separate stretch from start — every stretched
# child also starts at zero, so the classifier reports an ambiguity and
# refuses). The stills in the capture set are its only record today.
expect_root_fills
expect_fills textarea#0

# THE MENU BAR IS TWO GROUPS: File (New, Open, Save, Save As) and Edit
# (Undo, Redo, Cut, Copy, Paste, Find). Five of Edit's six items are
# kaya ROLES the app writes no behaviour for at all — which is the whole
# reason the forcing artifact is worth building.
expect_menus 2
# NOTHING HAS BEEN TYPED, so the platform's own undo has nothing to
# offer and the item says so. Enablement is not a build-time fact and
# this app computes none of it.
expect_menu "Edit>Undo" disabled

# ---- type into the empty buffer -----------------------------------
#
# The app puts the cursor in the document at mount, which is what an
# editor does and also what the Edit menu needs: undo, cut and paste all
# act on whatever is focused.
expect_focused textarea#0
type "alpha 1 beta 22"
expect textarea#0 "alpha 1 beta 22"
expect_dirty true
expect_menu "Edit>Undo" enabled

# ---- save as ------------------------------------------------------
#
# ARM THE DIRECTORY FIRST: a panel honors its initial location only AT
# presentation, and otherwise restores wherever it was last — which is
# how one run inherited a directory an unrelated binary left behind.
file_dialog_goto $TMP/kaya-editor-$PID
menu_activate "File>Save As…"
# The panel opens on the name the APP suggested; the harness types over
# it the way a person would, which is what a save dialog is for.
expect_save_dialog kaya-editor-$PID untitled
file_dialog_name draft
expect_save_dialog kaya-editor-$PID draft
file_save
# THE DESTINATION DID NOT EXIST — a desktop save panel answers with a
# name for a file nobody has made — and the byte count is READ BACK OFF
# THE DISK rather than being the length the app handed over. A write
# that returned success and landed nowhere is exactly the failure "save"
# has, and only reopening can see it.
expect label#0 "saved, 15 bytes"
# THE MARK COMES DOWN FIRST, AND THAT ORDER IS THE RULE rather than
# taste: on WinUI the unsaved-work asterisk rides the RENDERED CAPTION,
# which is what expect_title reads there, so a title read is only a
# five-lane byte comparison once the scene has CLAIMED the window clean.
# Every expect_title below therefore stands behind an expect_dirty
# false. tools/check-steps.sh enforces the shape now; it caught this
# exact stretch written the other way round.
expect_dirty false
# AND THE WINDOW TOOK THE FILE'S NAME. The destination moved, so the
# title moved with it — the status line says only what happened, and the
# name it used to carry is up in the chrome where a person looks for it.
expect_title "draft"

# ---- open a file --------------------------------------------------
#
# THE DECOY IS LOAD-BEARING: with one file in the directory a dialog
# completes with it when nothing is selected, so `file_choose` would
# pass on a backend that never selected anything. "decoy" sorts first,
# so that backend gets the WRONG file and every byte count below fails
# too.
file_dialog_goto $TMP/kaya-editor-$PID
menu_activate "File>Open…"
expect_file_dialog kaya-editor-$PID decoy draft notes
file_choose notes
expect label#0 "opened, 131 bytes"
expect_dirty false
# THE SECOND WAY A DESTINATION MOVES, and the title follows it exactly
# as it followed the save. `notes` is also the assertion that the DECOY
# was not opened: a backend that completed the picker with the
# first-sorted file would be titled `decoy` here.
expect_title "notes"

# ---- edit, and undo -----------------------------------------------
#
# TYPING IS REAL KEYSTROKES (`type`), never set_text: a programmatic
# write CLEARS a field's native undo history on every platform, which is
# the very history this step observes. Opening the document was such a
# write, so the run below is the only thing in that history.
#
# ONE CHARACTER, AND THE SCENE IS WRONG IF IT IS EVER MORE. How much of
# a typed run ONE undo spends is FRONTIER GRANULARITY — "the only
# platform-flavored part" of the undo design, which invariant 6 forbids a
# shared scene from asserting "across five lanes with five keystroke
# cadences" (docs/undo-plan.md §2 and §5.2). A one-character run is the
# floor every platform agrees on, and it is what undo.steps types for
# this same reason.
#
# THIS SHIPPED WRONG AND ONLY A SECOND CADENCE CAUGHT IT. The run here
# was ` tail`, and it passed on mac — AppKit coalesces a typing burst
# whole. On linux/x11 the same five characters arrive through
# `xdotool --delay 0` and GtkTextHistory spends ONE of them per undo, so
# the document came back four bytes long and every byte count below it
# failed. Wayland's `wtype -d 1` coalesced and passed. Same scene, same
# backend, two cadences, two answers — which is exactly what the rule
# says will happen. tools/check-steps.sh now refuses the shape.
expect_focused textarea#0
type "z"
expect_dirty true
# AND ONE UNDO PUTS THE DOCUMENT BACK, byte for byte. The app writes no
# undo stack, no command objects and no handlers for this: Edit>Undo is
# a ROLE, it routes to whatever is focused, and the field's own history
# answers. `expect_dirty false` is the strong form of the claim — the
# restored buffer equals the 131 bytes on disk exactly, or the mark
# would still be up.
menu_activate "Edit>Undo"
expect_dirty false

# ---- edit, and save to the file already chosen --------------------
#
# SAVE NEEDS NO DIALOG once a destination exists: the user already chose
# this file, and the handle they chose it with is writable.
expect_focused textarea#0
type " 99"
expect_dirty true
menu_activate "File>Save"
expect label#0 "saved, 134 bytes"
expect_dirty false
# THE TITLE DID NOT MOVE, which is the assertion with the negative in
# it: a Save that re-ran the panel, or that adopted some other handle,
# would rename the window here.
expect_title "notes"

# ---- summon the find bar ------------------------------------------
#
# THE BAR IS BUILT WHEN IT IS ASKED FOR, and the four widgets below did
# not exist a step ago: Edit>Find… writes a bool signal and kaya STAMPS
# the blueprint the app declared. That is the only conditional display
# kaya has — there is no visibility property and no way to take a live
# widget out of a tree — so "shown and hidden" (docs/editor-plan.md E1)
# means built and torn down, which is a stronger claim than hidden.
#
# THE TALLY IS THE PROOF THAT IT CAME UP. label#1 is a LIVE label in the
# status line, so its index never moves; it is empty while the bar is
# away and answers for the search while the bar is up. A backend that
# ignored the stamp would leave it empty here.
menu_activate "Edit>Find…"
expect label#1 "no matches"
expect entry#0 ""
#
# AND THE QUERY ARRIVES BY set_text, which is this script's one
# departure from its own real-keystrokes rule and not a convenience:
# NOTHING CAN FOCUS A STAMPED COPY. `focus` is a widget_command aimed at
# a live widget id, a copy's identity is (template node, key path), and
# `click` targets buttons — the same sentence tools/scenes/undo.steps
# already carries for a collection row's field. So the cursor stays in
# the document, the app cannot move it into a bar it can hide, and the
# scene writes the field instead of typing into it.
#
# THE HALF-TYPED REGEX IS THEREFORE SPELLED OUT rather than left to a
# keystroke cadence, which is the better shape anyway: find-as-you-type
# hands the app `[` before it hands it `[0-9]+`, and an app that treated
# a compile error as an incident would die on the first one. This is the
# state, named, and the app's answer to it asserted — which typing it
# could never do, since the intermediate states flew past unread.
set_text entry#0 "["
expect label#1 "bad pattern"
expect_highlights textarea#0 ""
set_text entry#0 "[0-9]+"
# THE MATCHES ARE FOUR RUNS OF DIFFERENT LENGTHS, so a backend that
# lowered one range's length onto all of them fails. The offsets are
# UTF-8 BYTE offsets into the app's own string — what Go's
# FindAllStringIndex answers in, what kaya.TextRange holds, and what the
# wire carries — and each assertion carries the text it covers as well
# as the numbers, because the core converts to the backend's unit to
# lower a range and back to read one: two symmetric mistakes would
# cancel, and offsets alone would pass while the wrong characters were
# decorated.
expect label#1 "1 of 4"
expect_highlights textarea#0 "4:5=7|66:68=13|129:131=42|132:134=99"
expect_selection textarea#0 "4:5=7"
# REVEAL, ASSERTED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. The `offscreen` line is what
# makes the `visible` one mean anything: the document is 59 lines, the
# last match is far below the window, and on a document that fits
# containment would be true before anything scrolled.
expect_revealed textarea#0 132:134 offscreen
# THE WALK IS DRIVEN THROUGH THE STAMPED BUTTONS, whose creation indices
# are stable HERE and only here: nothing has been torn down yet, so this
# first copy owns button#0 and button#1 on every lane. After a teardown
# a backend is not required to prune its harness registry (the SwiftUI
# interpreter does not, undo.steps:259), so every later step addresses
# the bar as `#last` — the newest stamped copy is the newest live one
# everywhere.
click button#1
click button#1
click button#1
expect label#1 "4 of 4"
expect_selection textarea#0 "132:134=99"
expect_revealed textarea#0 132:134 visible
# AND THE WALK WRAPS, both ways, which is the only behaviour a person
# ever notices about a find bar.
click button#1
expect label#1 "1 of 4"
click button#0
expect label#1 "4 of 4"

# ---- a declared set is bound to the text it was declared against ---
#
# The document changes, so kaya DROPS the decorations — it tracks
# nothing across an edit — and the app re-declares from the fold its
# change handler already drives. Here the re-declaration finds nothing,
# and the count says so rather than going stale.
set_text textarea#0 "scratch"
expect textarea#0 "scratch"
expect label#1 "no matches"
expect_highlights textarea#0 ""
expect_dirty true

# ---- dismiss it, and it is GONE -----------------------------------
#
# `done` is the bar's own door, because it cannot be a keystroke: kaya's
# shortcut floor reserves no bare Escape and an app cannot claim one. It
# is the last control the blueprint declares, so `#last` names it in any
# copy.
click button#last
# THE TALLY GOES QUIET and the cursor goes back to the document — the
# two things a person sees when a find bar leaves.
expect label#1 ""
expect_focused textarea#0
# AND THE PROOF THAT THE BAR WAS TORN DOWN RATHER THAN LEFT UP: summon
# it again and the field is EMPTY. Nothing in the app clears that field
# — dismissal forgets the app's own pattern and never touches the widget
# — so an empty one is a widget that did not exist a moment ago. A bar
# that had merely stayed mounted still reads "[0-9]+" here.
menu_activate "Edit>Find…"
expect entry#last ""
expect label#1 "no matches"
click button#last
expect label#1 ""
expect_order row#0 "saved, 134 bytes|"

# ---- the unsaved-work guard, from two different doors --------------
#
# Nothing in kaya says "ask before you discard": the app composes it out
# of parts that predate it — the alert machinery for New, and
# veto_close + close_requested for the window. Both refuse, both leave
# the work exactly where it was, and the second one proves the first
# dialog's id retired.
menu_activate "File>New"
expect_alert "unsaved changes"
alert_choose cancel
expect textarea#0 "scratch"
expect_dirty true
# AND THE DIALOG IS GONE BEFORE ANYTHING ASKS FOR ANOTHER — the wait
# that has to be spelled, MEASURED on the iOS lane 2026-08-10. A CANCEL
# whose continuation does nothing leaves no state for the next
# assertion to wait on: the two lines above were already true before the
# alert opened, so they passed in the same millisecond, the next
# `expect_alert` matched the alert STILL ON SCREEN (both doors carry the
# same title), and the app's second show hit the core's one-alert
# assertion —
#   kaya: alert 1 is already live — one alert per process
# — and aborted the guest. macOS dismisses fast enough to hide it; the
# simulator does not. This line is the only assertion that can hold here
# because it is about the DIALOG rather than about the document.
expect_alerts 0

# ---- and the answer that AGREES ------------------------------------
#
# Nothing in this script had ever taken the Discard arm: both doors
# above refuse, so the continuation the whole `ask` composition exists
# to run had never run. It is also the ONLY path that CLEARS the
# destination — save-as adopts one and open adopts one, and both are
# announced by a dialog the scene can read, while New drops one
# silently. The title is what makes it visible: the window goes back to
# the no-destination name, which is the third and last of the three
# ways a document's identity can move.
menu_activate "File>New"
expect_alert "unsaved changes"
alert_choose 0
expect textarea#0 ""
expect label#0 "new file"
expect_dirty false
expect_title "untitled"
# The same wait before the third door, for the same reason. Here the
# continuation DID change the document, so the four lines above are real
# synchronization — but the rule is about the shape and not about
# whether this particular stretch would have got away with it.
expect_alerts 0

# DIRTY AGAIN FOR THE SECOND DOOR, and this keystroke is load-bearing:
# a CLEAN document does not ask. The close would run straight through
# the guard into the affirmative arm, and that arm is os.Exit — the one
# call this app makes outside kaya (see editor.go's `quit`) — so the
# process would leave rather than the window. One character puts the
# state back that the next stretch is about.
expect_focused textarea#0
type "q"
expect_dirty true

close_window window#0
expect_alert "unsaved changes"
alert_choose cancel
expect_windows 1
expect_dirty true

# One REAL-TREE read at the end: everything above reads kaya's own
# model, and would pass for an arm that ran and drew nothing.
expect_ax label#0 "label/new file"
